<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chuquicamata</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chuquicamata is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mine near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelma">Calama</a> in the Atacama Desert of northern Chile, long the largest open-pit mine in the world by excavated volume. Developed by American companies from 1915, it was owned by the Anaconda Copper company until Chile nationalised copper in 1971. It is now operated by the state company <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codelco">Codelco</a>, partly as an underground mine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chuquicamata.jpg" alt="Chuquicamata" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mina_de_Chuquicamata,_Calama,_Chile,_2016-02-01,_DD_110-112_PAN.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
